root.Text:
{name} knows that this kind of game can be dangerous and decides to go beyond simply
 not wearing the mask... {he/she} buries it. {His/Her} faith is undeterred. {name}
 gains faith. You gain energy.

Unable to muster the courage to commit to the jump, {name} gives up on the idea. {He/She}
 feels ashamed for {his/her} lack of courage and ends up hating jumping in general
 because it reminds {him/her} of {his/her} failure.

One day, a heathen envoy comes to the believer center under a banner of peace. {He/She}
 claims to be interested in their ways and asks for a tour of their camp. {name} seems
 sincere, but it's possible that {he/she} is a spy.

{name} decides that the thing must certainly be a hat and promptly puts it on. {He/She}
 likes how it shades {his/her} eyes from the sun. However, {he/she} also notices that
 the heathens, who have a special love of headgear, greatly admire the hat. {name}
 uses this fact to {his/her} advantage when attempting to explain {his/her} point
 of view to the heathens.

{name} feels nothing at first, but the next time {he/she} sees a child playing, {name}
 begins to develop a very strong parenting instinct. {He/She} knows that it is {his/her}
 calling in life to raise children - lots and LOTS of children!
